    "description": "Student migration connects Iran's higher education system with universities and research careers abroad. Destination-country student counts document part of that movement, but they do not directly measure annual departures from Iran, permanent emigration, or the proportion of students who return.",
    "tags": ["Student migration", "International education", "Iranian students", "Academic mobility", "Brain drain"],
    "status": null,
    "editorial_status": "ready_for_review",
    "as_of_date": "2026-10-26",
    "created_at": "2026-10-27T18:05:06Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-27T18:05:06Z",
    "seo": {
      "title": "Student Migration from Iran: Evidence and Measurement",
      "description": "Understand Iranian student mobility, U.S. academic-year counts, and why studying abroad, annual departures, and permanent emigration require different evidence."
    },
    "content": [
      {
        "id": "mobility-and-migration",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Studying abroad and leaving permanently",
        "content": "Student migration is a movement through education that may overlap with longer-term migration, but the two are not equivalent. A person studying overseas may return, continue to another destination, or enter employment abroad. Counting students at foreign institutions does not establish which of these outcomes follows. The Stanford Iran 2040 Project's research on migration and IIE's destination-country statistics address different parts of this larger picture. [s1][s2]\n\nThe Stanford study places student mobility within the history of Iranian migration and the international distribution of scholars. Its account describes changing educational migration over time and discusses graduate scholarships as one route that can reduce financial barriers to study abroad. This is a historical research interpretation, published in 2020, rather than a current census of students or a direct measure of present-day motivations. [s1]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2"]
      },
      {
        "id": "definitions-matter",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "Who is being counted?",
        "content": "Definitions are essential when comparing sources. The Stanford research discusses Iranian-born students and scholars of Iranian descent, whereas Open Doors organizes its international student statistics by place of origin. These categories should not be silently combined into a single worldwide total. A person already living abroad before university also has a different migration history from someone who leaves Iran specifically to study. [s1][s2]\n\nOpen Doors, published by the Institute of International Education, counts international students associated with U.S. higher education, including participants in Optional Practical Training, a category of practical work experience. Consequently, its total is broader than a count of people currently taking classes and narrower geographically than a worldwide count of Iranian students. It is also an academic-year population measure, not the number of new departures from Iran during that year. [s2][s3]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "reading-us-series",
        "type": "text",
        "heading": "What the U.S. series shows",
        "content": "The accompanying table preserves the academic-year observations published in the Iran country fact sheet. It shows a decline followed by recovery, with the latest observation still below the first observation displayed. These totals describe one destination and cannot establish whether worldwide Iranian student mobility followed the same path. [s2]\n\nComparability also requires a warning. During the pandemic, Open Doors broadened its definition to include students studying remotely from outside the United States. Beginning with the 2024 report, it excluded that group and returned to its pre-pandemic definition. The underlying table therefore contains a documented coverage change, and differences across that transition should not be interpreted as pure changes in physical mobility. [s3]\n\nFor assessing consequences in Iran, overseas enrollment is a starting point. Evidence on return, onward movement, subsequent employment, and continuing research connections is needed to distinguish temporary study from lasting loss or circulation of expertise. The historical study explores the wider migration question, but the student-count dataset below cannot resolve those outcomes or explain individual decisions. [s1][s2]",
        "source_ids": ["s1", "s2", "s3"]
      },
      {
        "id": "us-student-counts",
        "type": "table",
        "content": {
          "title": "Iran-origin international students in the United States, by academic year; coverage changes noted",
          "dataset_id": "iran-us-academic-year-counts"
        },
        "source_ids": ["s2", "s3"]
      }
    ],
    "datasets": [
      {
